From 2cf280c7610aee5f92f3d2e6507c336e2c620726 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Tue, 22 Sep 2026 12:01:47 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] fix(payments): enforce assignment scope and loan-state, map
 errors to HTTP status
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Four coordinated fixes to postManualPayment and its route, ordered so the
existing idempotency check runs first — a retry of an already-posted
payment must succeed even when the loan or assignment state that same
payment changed would now fail the new checks:

1. Object-level assignment scope. A collector/officer could alter loanId
   in the request body and post a payment against any loan in their
   branch — collector_assignments was never consulted. Now enforced for
   actorRole 'collector'/'officer' only (admin/manager bypass, matching
   the existing pattern in the collector-reporting route); skipped
   entirely when actorRole is omitted (internal/seed callers, which don't
   seed assignment rows).

2. Loan lifecycle status. loan.status was fetched but never checked
   against anything. Now gated on PAYABLE_LOAN_STATUSES = {active,
   overdue, disbursed, defaulted}, derived directly from loanTransitions
   in state-machines.ts: 'approved' (undisbursed, no debt yet) and the
   terminal 'written_off'/'completed' are excluded; 'defaulted' stays
   payable because loanTransitions itself allows defaulted -> active,
   which is exactly what a recovery payment does.

3. Idempotency-before-state ordering. Both new checks are inserted after
   the existing idempotency lookup, not before, specifically so a retried
   payment that already completed the loan (or outlived a since-lapsed
   assignment window) still returns the original result instead of
   throwing LOAN_NOT_PAYABLE / COLLECTOR_NOT_ASSIGNED.

4. Error -> HTTP status mapping. Every business-rule error the service
   throws fell through the route's bare `throw error` to the global
   error handler, which has no way to tell a permanent business failure
   from a genuine server fault and returns a generic 500. The offline
   sync queue's retry classification (network/401/5xx => retry, else
   stop and flag) then retried these forever. Route now maps each known
   error code to its real status (400/403/404/409) via a lookup table;
   only genuinely unknown errors still fall through to the 500 handler.

Adds 8 regression tests: two LOAN_NOT_PAYABLE cases, one defaulted-loan
pass-through, one idempotent-retry-past-completion, one unassigned-collector
rejection, one assigned-collector/admin-bypass pass-through, one
idempotent-retry-past-assignment-lapse (this one initially written wrong —
it accidentally passed while testing a different, mislabeled scenario;
corrected to actually seed a real first payment then lapse the assignment
before retrying, which caught a genuine CHECK-constraint seeding bug in the
test itself, not the fix), and one HTTP-level app.inject test proving a
business error now returns 409 with a real error code instead of 500.
